TASK 2 — Users, Groups, and Password Policy  (20 pts)
Topic: groupadd, useradd, passwd, chage, sudoers
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
You are provisioning accounts for a new operations team member.

Note: the ops group (GID 50100) already exists — do not recreate it.

1. Create a user named sysadmin1 with ops as a supplementary group.
2. Set the initial password to Welcome1.
3. Force a password change on first login.
4. Set the following password policy for sysadmin1:
   - Minimum days between password changes: 7
   - Maximum days before password change required: 90
   - Account expiry date: 2027-06-30
5. Allow sysadmin1 to run any command as root using sudo, without a password
   prompt. Use a dedicated file in /etc/sudoers.d/.

Hint: useradd -G adds supplementary groups. passwd sets the password.
      chage -d 0 forces a change on next login. chage -m, -M, -E set
      the policy values. The sudoers file line format is:
        sysadmin1 ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL


TASK 3 — File Permissions and Collaborative Directory  (20 pts)
Topic: mkdir, chown, chmod, SGID, sticky bit, umask
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The ops team needs a shared workspace.

1. Create the directory /home/ops-workspace.
2. Set the group owner to ops.
3. Set permissions so that:
   - Owner has rwx
   - Group members have rwx
   - All other users have no access (---)
4. Set the SGID bit so that new files inherit the ops group automatically.
5. Set the sticky bit so users can only delete their own files.
6. Configure sysadmin1's shell so that umask is persistently set to 007
   (add the line to /home/sysadmin1/.bashrc or .bash_profile).

Expected final permissions on the directory: drwxrws--T

Hint: chmod 3770 sets rwxrws--T in one shot (SGID=2000, sticky=1000).
      The sticky bit shows as T (not t) when group execute is not set.
      umask 007 means new files get 770 permissions by default.


